s 16÷8\n$$\n\nExpressing the division as a fraction, we arrive at:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\frac{16}{8}\n$$\n\nWe now solve:\n\n$$\n5\\times {{\\frac{16}{8}}_{⏟}}_{2}=5\\times 2=10\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-afd932c3-22eb-5ef3-88e0-b09b1b4421be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-afd932c3-22eb-5ef3-88e0-b09b1b4421be" :answer-field/id #uuid "afd932c3-22eb-5ef3-88e0-b09b1b4421be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-415ab599-15f8-591b-8e98-ca2def108840" :answer-field/choices ["answer-415ab599-15f8-591b-8e98-ca2def108840" "answer-291b714b-83d7-5e1a-9c1b-82a87f35d18b" "answer-1aa6fbd8-4fcf-5925-9c20-588f6c9546c1" "answer-0d087be9-1e14-5a8e-b384-ec499b195a67" "answer-c2b615d3-5940-508f-9b2e-a5627bfadf8c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-415ab599-15f8-591b-8e98-ca2def108840" :answer/id #uuid "415ab599-15f8-591b-8e98-ca2def108840" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-291b714b-83d7-5e1a-9c1b-82a87f35d18b" :answer/id #uuid "291b714b-83d7-5e1a-9c1b-82a87f35d18b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1aa6fbd8-4fcf-5925-9c20-588f6c9546c1" :answer/id #uuid "1aa6fbd8-4fcf-5925-9c20-588f6c9546c1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d087be9-1e14-5a8e-b384-ec499b195a67" :answer/id #uuid "0d087be9-1e14-5a8e-b384-ec499b195a67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c2b615d3-5940-508f-9b2e-a5627bfadf8c" :answer/id #uuid "c2b615d3-5940-508f-9b2e-a5627bfadf8c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148776 :tutorial/content "Remember that every fraction represents a division: the numerator divided by the denominator. \n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{3} = 5 \\div 3.\n$$\n\nWe can use this idea to rewrite expressions containing fractions.\n\nFor example, suppose we want to rewrite the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}3}\\times {\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nInterpreting the fraction as division, we can write our expression as\n\n$$\n5{\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3}{\\color{blue}\\;\\times\\; 12}.\n$$\n\nNext, we use the fact that we can swap the order of multiplication and division. So, we can write an equivalent expression by switching the $\\color{red}\\div\\, 3$ and the ${\\color{blue}\\times\\, 12},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n5{\\color{blue}\\;\\times\\; 12}{\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}12} = 5{\\color{blue}\\;\\times\\; 12}{\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148777 :tutorial/content "Consider the expression $\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}12}.$ By expressing the fraction as division, we have\n\n$$\n5 {\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3} {\\color{blue}\\;\\times\\; 12},\n$$\n\nand by swapping the ${\\color{red}\\div\\; 3}$ and the ${\\color{blue}\\times\\; 12},$ we have\n\n$$\n5 {\\color{blue}\\;\\times\\; 12}{\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that we can rewrite the division ${\\color{blue}12} {\\color{red}\\;\\div\\; 3}$ as a new fraction $\\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3}$ to produce a new expression:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}12} = 5\\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186148778 :tutorial/content "Let's evaluate the following expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{\\color{red}3} \\times {\\color{blue}12}\n$$\n\nFirst, by swapping the order of multiplication and division, we can rewrite our expression as\n\n$$\n5\\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3}.\n$$\n\nNow, notice that ${\\color{red}3}$ is a factor of ${\\color{blue}{12}},$ and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3} = {\\color{blue}{12}} \\div {\\color{red}3}  = 4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our entire expression as follows:\n\n$$\n5\\times \\underbrace{\\dfrac{\\color{blue}12}{\\color{red}3}}_{4} = 5 \\times 4 = 20\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186147659 :activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
